[[[42, 85, 57, 194], 'header', 'CHI 2019 Paper'], [[42, 582, 58, 913], 'header', 'CHI 2019, May 4-9, 2019, Glasgow, Scotland, UK'], [[118, 83, 253, 483], 'para', 'In order to interpret hints, the AI assumes its teammate
uses the same process of reasoning and therefore the same
hint strategy. The AI can then use the decision tree as a way
to simulate its teammate\'s mental model and make sense of
the hints it receives. Figure 3 depicts the way the AI simulates
its teammate\'s thought process to determine whether there
is implication behind the hint [84] . The AI is unable to see
its own hand and therefore relies entirely on the hints its
teammate provides to narrow down the identity of its cards.'], [[253, 85, 480, 483], 'para', 'The AI knows for each card in its hand which colors and
which numbers are possible given the current state of the
game. When the AI receives a hint, it considers each possible
hand and the flow of the decision tree in Figure 2 to determine
what kind of hint it would give if its teammate was the one
with that possible hand. The AI can then use this process
to determine the likely identities of the cards in its hand by
checking if the hint it would have given aligns with the hint
it actually received from its teammate. For example, if the AI
receives the hint "you have one 2", then only the possibility
of the hinted card being actionable aligns with this hint. All
possible hands that would result in this hint being generated
require that the card be playable. Therefore the AI interprets
the hint it received as "play this card."'], [[520, 85, 537, 162], 'sec_1', 'Entropy AI'], [[540, 85, 582, 483], 'para', 'As a baseline comparison to the implication AI, we created
an entropy-minimization AI that reflects the hinting strategy
of prior work without introducing confounding factors.'], [[582, 85, 670, 483], 'para', 'An obvious strategy for Hanabi is to view one\'s partner\'s
ignorance about the identity of his cards as the primary
obstacle. The more a player knows about his own hand, the
more likely he can make an informed decision about the
appropriate move to take with it.'], [[670, 85, 743, 483], 'para', 'We can think of this strategy in terms of entropy mini-
mization. The less one knows about a card, the higher the
entropy, so attempting to minimize entropy in the context of
Hanabi is equivalent to trying to inform one\'s partner about
as many cards as possible per hint.'], [[743, 85, 775, 483], 'para', 'For example, suppose in Figure 1 that Bernard is the one
with the visible hand and Annie has to provide information:'], [[788, 100, 898, 484], 'list', '(1) Annie wants Bernard to be able to make the best move
he can from his own knowledge.
(2) Bernard has no knowledge about his current hand.
(3) Annie\'s goal is to maximize the difference in knowl-
edge between his current hand and his hand at the
next turn. That is, she seeks to minimize how much
he does not know as of the next turn.
(4) The maximal entropy-reduction hint Annie can give
Bernard is about blue cards (since he has three).'], [[118, 515, 205, 914], 'para', 'We observe that the entropy strategy fundamentally in-
volves the player with more knowledge (the hint-giver) en-
trusting her partner to make a good decision about which
card to play. The bottleneck is the lack of information about
the identities of the hint recipient\'s cards.'], [[205, 515, 300, 914], 'para', 'A shortcoming of this hint strategy is that hints intended
only to minimize entropy are unnatural for human Hanabi
players as they are not actionable information. If Bernard
receives the hint that he has three blue cards, he will not
know what to do with those blue cards and therefore cannot
derive his next move from this hint alone.'], [[311, 515, 327, 750], 'sec_1', '6 EVALUATION HYPOTHESES'], [[330, 515, 361, 913], 'para', 'The goal of our study is to address two hypotheses about the
use of implication in Hanabi.'], [[361, 515, 419, 914], 'list', 'H1. Teams playing with the implication AI achieve overall
better scores than teams playing with the entropy AI.
H2. Humans will perceive the implication AI to be more
human-like than the entropy AI.'], [[430, 515, 447, 810], 'sec_1', '7 EVALUATION WITH AI-AI GAMES'], [[450, 515, 481, 913], 'para', 'This section describes a simulation-only study designed to
explore hypothesis H1.'], [[492, 515, 508, 762], 'sec_2', 'Simulation Setup and Execution'], [[511, 515, 592, 914], 'para', 'We evaluated each hint-giving strategy described above by
teaming each AI with a copy of itself. We ran 1000 trial games
with the implication AI-AI team and 1000 trial games with
the entropy AI-AI team, using final score as our metric for
collaborative effectiveness. H1 predicts a higher average final
score for the implication AI than for the entropy AI.'], [[612, 513, 836, 917], 'fig', '![Figure](figures/3290605-3300835_pdf_shortened_page_6_figure_014.png)', 'This bar chart displays the "Number of games" on the y-axis against "Score" on the x-axis, ranging from 0 to 25. Two categories, "implicate" (red bars) and "entropy" (blue bars), are shown for each score, with the blue bars generally appearing higher than the red bars.'], [[848, 515, 892, 913], 'figure_cap', 'Figure 4: Final scores of games played by an AI-AI team em-
ploying either the implication-based strategy (red) or the
entropy-minimization strategy (blue).'], [[943, 85, 959, 154], 'foot', 'Paper 95'], [[943, 865, 959, 913], 'foot', 'Page 7']]